  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "dom_id": "question-113970",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/76d3a2d884ab6df8d6b6b97be5d5bac91ab30976ba0a6ef8aacc5f3a30eead6c.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $M,T,N$\n\nII. $S,T,O$\n\nIII. $P,Q,N$",
      "worked_solution": "- $M,T,N$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/a3574ff790eadbc561d842f7c2b485d055a76f5781da76afbd7fb39e45e7ccf0.png)\n\n- $S,T,O$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/4ca9c5bbfc7c0f95a5bc03e6e84102eb362cba63d937a184089584a7c2ad58bf.png)\n\n- $P,Q,N$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220930/assets/6b5e9a5c1e1fa271c93667c35c117b197cec3d5a25e5ac311e77f0021663163d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "result": "Incorrect",
